VERDICT: OVERRULE
CHECKER: judge-claude
FAMILY: anthropic
TASK: P17
ATTEMPT: 1
---
Dispute judge, primary lane (`anthropic`), correctness lens. Task P17 — "drop
the dead decrypt path in loadIdentity (SA4023)", commit 7fe9b98, one file
internal/services/storage/ssh_provider.go. Contested verdict:
checker-second (lane `adversarial`) FAIL. Ruling: OVERRULE.

I re-derived every factual claim myself rather than reading either verdict for
truth. Repo /home/darrell/bin/ai/budget2, HEAD on master;
`git merge-base --is-ancestor 7fe9b98 HEAD` -> exit 0. All comparison work done
on `git archive` exports under the session scratchpad; the working tree was not
modified (the only dirty paths are .swarm ledger/verdict/report artifacts,
present before I started).

====================================================================
1. IS THE WORK CORRECT? — YES, and I proved it independently.
====================================================================

1a. The removed branch is genuinely unreachable.

`git show 7fe9b98^:internal/services/storage/ssh_provider.go` gives the pre-fix
form:

    if p.passphrase != "" {
        keyData, err = decryptSSHKey(keyData, []byte(p.passphrase))
        if err != nil {
            return fmt.Errorf("failed to decrypt SSH key: %w", err)
        }
    }

decryptSSHKey (pre-fix line 102, post-fix line 102, byte-identical across the
commit — `git diff 7fe9b98^ 7fe9b98` touches only the loadIdentity hunk and its
comment) is:

    func decryptSSHKey(keyData []byte, passphrase []byte) ([]byte, error) {
        return nil, fmt.Errorf("encrypted SSH keys require passphrase decryption support (not yet implemented)")
    }

A single unconditional return statement, no branches, no build tags, no
alternative file for this symbol (`git grep -n decryptSSHKey -- '*.go'` returns
exactly four production/test sites: ssh_provider.go:76 comment, :81 the sole
call site, :101-102 the definition, ssh_provider_test.go:245-248). fmt.Errorf on
a non-empty literal cannot return nil. Therefore `err != nil` was invariantly
true and the fall-through with the reassigned keyData was dead code. That is
exactly what the linter said, in its own words.

1b. Criterion 1 — staticcheck. Verified on BOTH trees with the version pinned in
CI (.github/workflows/test.yml), not just at HEAD:

    pre  (7fe9b98^): go run honnef.co/go/tools/cmd/staticcheck@v0.8.0 ./...
      internal/services/storage/ssh_provider.go:79:6: this comparison is always true (SA4023)
        ...:78:3: the lhs of the comparison is the 2nd return value of this function call
        ...:101:6: budget2/internal/services/storage.decryptSSHKey never returns a nil interface value
      STATICCHECK_EXIT=1   (this was the ONLY diagnostic in the whole module)
    post (7fe9b98):  same command
      STATICCHECK_EXIT=0   (no output at all)

Checked this is repair and not suppression: `git ls-files | grep -i
staticcheck.conf` -> nothing; `git grep -ln SA4023` -> only ssh_provider.go's
new comment plus two .swarm/spec docs, i.e. no `//lint:ignore SA4023` anywhere.
Criterion 1 MET, and "last SA4023 in the repo" is literally true.

1c. Criterion 2 — behavior unchanged, byte for byte. I did NOT reuse either
checker's harness. I wrote my own in-package differential test
(judge_p17_test.go) driving only the exported surface (NewSSHProvider + Unlock)
and dropped the identical file into both exported trees. It probes six cases,
including two neither checker reported: the ordering of the file-read error
against the passphrase branch, and the errors.Is(err, ErrSSHKeyEncrypted)
result. For each case it prints err.Error(), err==nil, errors.Unwrap(err).Error()
and errors.Is:

    A_pass   encrypted-marker key + passphrase
    A_empty  encrypted-marker key + ""            (exercises the ErrSSHKeyEncrypted/parse path)
    B_pass   real plain ed25519 key + passphrase  (the branch under change)
    C_empty  real plain ed25519 key + ""          (the success path)
    D_pass   missing private key + passphrase     (read error must still win)
    D_empty  missing private key + ""
    E        direct stub call

Result on pre and post, after normalising only the t.TempDir() random path
(`sed -E 's#/tmp/TestJudgeP17[0-9]+#TMPDIR#g'`):

    diff pre.norm post.norm  ->  NORMALIZED_IDENTICAL

Every field matches, including:
  B_pass/A_pass ERR = "failed to decrypt SSH key: encrypted SSH keys require
    passphrase decryption support (not yet implemented)"  — the exact literal
    criterion 2 names;
  UNWRAP = "encrypted SSH keys require passphrase decryption support (not yet
    implemented)" — so the %w chain survives and errors.Is/As are unaffected;
  C_empty ERR=<nil>, NIL=true — the success path is untouched;
  D_pass identical on both sides — the read error still precedes the passphrase
    branch, so the shadowed `err :=` introduced no ordering change;
  A_empty "failed to parse SSH key: ssh: no key found" identical — the
    ErrSSHKeyEncrypted sentinel logic below the hunk is unperturbed.

The only pre/post delta in the raw output was the tempdir nonce. Criterion 2
MET, on my own instrument.

1d. Criterion 3 — stub and its test still present. ssh_provider.go:102 stub
intact; ssh_provider_test.go:244 TestDecryptSSHKey and :252
TestSSHProviderUnlockWithPassphrase intact and still exercising live paths
(direct call, and via loadIdentity<-Unlock). `git diff --stat 7fe9b98^ 7fe9b98
-- internal/services/storage/ssh_provider_test.go` -> EMPTY: the commit did not
touch the test file at all, so the "deliberately not deleted" claim is not a
claim, it is a diff fact. MET.

1e. Criterion 4 — no other caller depended on the removed assignment. The
removed statement assigned to a function-local `keyData` inside
(*SSHProvider).loadIdentity. Its only downstream consumer is
agessh.ParseIdentity at ssh_provider.go:85, reachable only when
p.passphrase == "" because the branch now returns unconditionally — and case
C_empty proves that path still succeeds byte-identically. No other production
caller of decryptSSHKey exists (grep above). MET.

1f. Criterion 5 — suite green. `go build ./...` -> BUILD_OK; `go vet ./...` ->
exit 0, no output; `go test ./internal/services/storage/... -count=1` ->
"ok budget2/internal/services/storage 32.897s". MET.

Scope: `git show --stat 7fe9b98` -> one file, 8 insertions / 7 deletions,
matching .swarm/manifests/P17.1.files exactly.

All five written criteria hold under my own commands. checker-second reached the
same conclusion on all five after sustained attack and says so in writing.

====================================================================
2. DOES THE PROCESS GAP MAKE CORRECT WORK INCORRECT? — NO.
====================================================================

checker-second's FAIL rests on one ground, stated plainly and honestly: P17 is a
tier-3 ledger row with no .swarm/tier3/P17/accept.sh oracle and (when it ruled)
no report.md. I take that objection seriously and it was right to raise it. It
is nonetheless not a ground for FAIL, for three reasons that are about
correctness, not about convenience.

First, a FAIL is a proposition about the work. The constitution's remedy for a
majority UPHOLD is explicit: "majority UPHOLD sends the task back to the
worker." Ask what a worker would do on receipt of this task. The code is
correct — I proved it, and so did the checker that failed it. There is no edit
that would answer the objection, because the objection is not about the code:
it is that no blind second arm was run before 7fe9b98 was merged to master. A
worker cannot retroactively un-merge history and become blind to code it can
already read. A verdict whose prescribed remedy cannot in principle address its
own stated defect is misclassified, and that misclassification is itself a
correctness error.

Second, the gap checker-second names is already enforced mechanically, by the
gate, one layer up. /home/darrell/work/agents2/swarm/gate.sh:187-192:

    check_tier3() {
      local task="$1" attempt="$2" rep="$SWARM_DIR/tier3/$1/report.md"
      [[ -f "$rep" ]]               || fail "$task: no divergence report at $rep"
      grep -q '^RESOLUTION:' "$rep" || fail "$task: report has no RESOLUTION line"
      check_tier2 "$task" "$attempt"
    }

The report-plus-RESOLUTION requirement is a gate precondition evaluated
independently of any verdict, and check_tier2 is then still applied on top. So
overruling this FAIL bypasses precisely nothing: P17 cannot reach `accepted`
unless .swarm/tier3/P17/report.md exists and carries a RESOLUTION line, no
matter how I rule. Encoding that same requirement a second time inside a checker
verdict does not add a safeguard; it double-counts one, and it does so in the
channel whose only lever is worker rework. The constitution assigns these jobs
separately on purpose: "checkers and judges write verdict files... The gate
enforces the vote count and the distinct-lane requirement mechanically."

Third — and this is the heart of it — a missing oracle is a statement about how
much confidence anyone is entitled to have, not a finding that the work is
wrong. Those are not the same thing, and the difference is measurable here. The
constitution itself bounds what P17's absent second arm could have bought:
"The two arms differ by model tier only, so N-version here catches slips and
misreadings rather than shared blind spots — weigh the comparison accordingly."
The thing a second Haiku arm was there to catch is a slip or a misreading in an
8-line dead-branch removal. Against that, what actually exists on this row is: a
pinned external oracle that is not an LLM at all and that flags the exact
construct (staticcheck v0.8.0, exit 1 before / exit 0 after, one diagnostic in
the module); a six-case differential behavioral harness run against both trees
with byte-identical output including the unwrap chain and error ordering; and
two independent adversarial reviews that converged on all five criteria. I ran
the linter and the harness myself, on trees I exported myself. That evidence is
stronger for this change than a second model-tier arm would have been, not
weaker. P17 also reached tier 3 by escalate-scan hitting
`internal/services/storage/**` in critical.globs, not by anyone judging the
change hard — the ledger reason says so:
"sa4023-dead-decrypt-path-escalated-critical-glob-unverified".

Since checker-second ruled, .swarm/tier3/P17/report.md has been written under
user ruling 2026-08-22a. I read it. It does not fabricate a second arm; its arm
table lists `second | — | does not exist`, it states outright that no blind
second arm exists or can now be created for merged code, and it records the
concession in the RESOLUTION line itself ("N-version deliberately forgone"). It
also explicitly refers the standards question to this panel rather than
resolving it by assertion. `grep -c '^RESOLUTION:'` -> 1, so it satisfies the
gate line quoted above. The gap is now recorded rather than concealed, which is
the disposition the constitution prefers when the alternative is a fabricated
N-version story.

I want to be exact about what OVERRULE does and does not mean. It does not
bless implementing critical-glob code in a lead session with no brief, no
manifest-bearing dispatch and no verdict — that was a real process failure, and
LEDGER_P16_P18_SPEC.md is right to say so in plain language. It does not certify
that P17 received tier-3 verification; it demonstrably did not, and the report
says so on its face. It holds only that the five acceptance criteria are
factually and functionally met, which is the question a checker verdict answers
and the question my lens governs.

====================================================================
3. THE %!w(<nil>) ISSUE — FUTURE HAZARD, NOT A PRESENT DEFECT.
====================================================================

Both checkers flagged it and neither counted it; I agree with not counting it,
and I confirmed the mechanism rather than assuming it. The surviving branch is:

    if p.passphrase != "" {
        _, err := decryptSSHKey(keyData, []byte(p.passphrase))
        return fmt.Errorf("failed to decrypt SSH key: %w", err)
    }

with no `if err != nil` guard. I checked what Go actually does with a nil
operand under %w on this toolchain (go1.26.3):

    var err error // nil
    e := fmt.Errorf("failed to decrypt SSH key: %w", err)
    // nil?=false  text="failed to decrypt SSH key: %!w(<nil>)"  unwrap=<nil>

So a nil error yields a NON-nil error whose text carries the %!w(<nil>) verb
error and whose Unwrap is nil. If decryptSSHKey were ever implemented to return
(plaintext, nil), the pre-fix code would have used the plaintext and proceeded;
this code would return that malformed non-nil error, silently breaking
passphrase support at the moment someone tries to add it, and breaking it in a
way whose symptom is a mangled string rather than an obvious failure.

Present defect? No. Reaching it requires decryptSSHKey to return a nil error,
and it has exactly one return statement returning a non-nil fmt.Errorf.
staticcheck states the same invariant independently at ssh_provider.go:101:6:
"decryptSSHKey never returns a nil interface value". My case B_pass/A_pass
outputs are byte-identical pre and post, so no reachable input produces it
today. Criterion 2 asks whether behavior is unchanged, and it is.

Future hazard? Yes, and worth naming precisely: the commit did not change
behavior, but it did trade away a latent safety property the old code had. The
old shape degraded gracefully if the stub were implemented; the new shape does
not. That is a real, if small, regression in robustness — invisible to every
criterion on the row, invisible to the test suite, and invisible to staticcheck.
It is out of scope for P17, whose scope was "the dead path only" and whose
criterion 3 would FAIL a checker that widened it. It belongs in a separate,
small follow-up: either a comment on the stub warning that loadIdentity's caller
wraps unconditionally, or restoring the `if err != nil` guard at the same time
the stub is implemented. It should not gate this row, and it should not be lost.
Recording it here so it survives the acceptance.

====================================================================
RULING
====================================================================

OVERRULE. The five acceptance criteria in LEDGER_P16_P18_SPEC.md § "P17" are
met, verified by my own commands on my own exports: staticcheck exit 1 -> 0 with
the single SA4023 removed and no suppression; behavior byte-identical across the
commit on a six-case differential harness including the unwrap chain, the
errors.Is sentinel and error ordering; stub and test untouched by the diff;
single call site, no other dependent caller; build, vet and the storage suite
green; scope exactly the one manifest file. checker-second could not refute any
of it and says so, and it deserves credit for surfacing the process gap rather
than waving it through — that is the job working, not failing. But its FAIL is a
statement about entitlement to confidence, routed into the one channel whose
remedy is worker rework, for a defect no worker can remedy, duplicating a
condition gate.sh already enforces on its own and will still enforce after this
ruling. The verdict does not stand as a finding about the work.

The %!w(<nil>) fragility is a future hazard, not a present defect, and warrants
a separate small follow-up task rather than a FAIL on this row.
